<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!--
    The play wedge: Ripcord's action language, and on a console card it IS the primary action rather than an
    icon sitting inside a button.

    Two shapes, one control:

      the zone   a slanted band at the trailing edge, leaning forward like an italic. It is what makes "the
                 whole card is one focus stop" visually true - the card does not need a Connect button
                 because its trailing edge already says the card launches something.
      the mark   the play triangle from brand/ripcord-mark.svg, "M29 17 L53 32 L29 47 Z" with a round join.
                 The coordinates here are that path, translated to sit in the zone. brand/*.svg is the source
                 of truth; keep this in step with it and do not re-draw the triangle by eye.

    Three rules from docs/design.md keep it from becoming decoration, and all three are enforced here rather
    than by whoever uses the control:

      - A wedge means "this launches something." Never navigation.
      - It scales with its container. 92px beside a 292x188 grid card, 184px beside the one-console hero - a
        proportion of the card, not a chip with a fixed size. Hence the Viewbox: callers set a width and the
        geometry follows, so there is exactly one set of coordinates in the app.
      - The diagonal is the loudest thing in the app. It stays on Ripcord's own surfaces and never reaches a
        settings row.

    ON THE UNREACHABLE CASE, WHICH DEPARTS FROM THE WRITTEN DESIGN - see Muted.

    docs/design.md says a console that cannot be reached has "no wedge at all - the affordance is missing
    rather than greyed". That was written on 2026-09-13, when "cannot be reached" was believed to be
    knowledge. It is not: a probe is one UDP datagram, and on 2026-09-19 a console that was powered on for an
    entire session was reported unreachable because that datagram went missing. Removing the affordance
    entirely would rebuild, in the visual layer, the dead end that was just removed from the logic layer.

    So the wedge stays and goes quiet instead: the zone drops to a neutral fill and loses its accent. That
    keeps the doc's actual REASONING - an unreachable console is a normal state and not a fault, so no red,
    no error glyph, nothing that reads as broken - while leaving the player something to press. The design
    doc records this amendment; if the two ever disagree again, the doc is authoritative and this comment is
    the thing to correct.
-->
<UserControl
    x:Class="Ripcord_App.Controls.PlayWedge"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
    xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
    mc:Ignorable="d">

    <!--
        Decorative to assistive technology. The card that hosts this composes its own AutomationName from the
        console's name, state and what activating it will do, so announcing the wedge as well would add a
        second voice saying "play" after the sentence that already said it.
    -->
    <Viewbox
        AutomationProperties.AccessibilityView="Raw"
        Stretch="Uniform">
        <Canvas Width="92" Height="176">

            <!--
                The zone. Leading edge runs from (28,0) to (0,176) - further right at the top, so it leans
                the way italics lean and reads as forward motion rather than as a corner that got cut.
            -->
            <Path Data="M 28,0 L 92,0 L 92,176 L 0,176 Z" Fill="{x:Bind ZoneFill, Mode=OneWay}" />

            <!--
                The play triangle, brand geometry, centred in the parallel part of the zone. The round join
                is the mark's own (stroke-width 6, stroke-linejoin round in the SVG) and is what keeps it
                from reading as a sharp arrowhead.
            -->
            <Path
                Data="M 40,73 L 64,88 L 40,103 Z"
                Fill="{x:Bind MarkFill, Mode=OneWay}"
                Stroke="{x:Bind MarkFill, Mode=OneWay}"
                StrokeLineJoin="Round"
                StrokeThickness="6" />
        </Canvas>
    </Viewbox>
</UserControl>
